    "lawCode": "Add §18-e, Gen City L",
    "lawSection": "General City Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S3848\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the general city law, in relation to prohibiting the\ndestruction of information submitted by applicants for municipal forms\nof identification, and requiring such information to be submitted to\nthe division of homeland security and emergency services and the\noffice for new Americans\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThis bill requires cities which issue forms of identification to\nmaintain the application information relating thereto, and submit it\nto the Division of Homeland Security and Emergency Services and the\nOffice for New Americans.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one amends the general city law by adding a new section 18-e\nregarding forms of identification records. Subdivision one states that\nno city that issues any form of identification to persons, who are not\nemployees of such city, other than identification used solely as proof\nof residency in such city, shall destroy or cause to be destroyed any\napplication, or the information contained therein, for such form of\nidentification. Any city that violates the provisions of this\nsubdivision will be subject to a civil penalty of $1000 for each\napplication or portion thereof that was destroyed. The proceeds of all\npenalties collected pursuant to this subdivision shall be allocated\nand disbursed to the Office of New Americans to carry out its purpose\npursuant to § 94b of the executive law. Subdivision two states that\nwithin ten days of receipt of an application for a form of\nidentification issued by a city, such city shall forward copies of\nsuch application to the Division of Homeland Security and Emergency\nServices and the Office for New Americans.\n\nSection two states that every city which issued any form of\nidentification, to which section 18-e of the general city law, as\nadded by section one of this act applies, prior to the effective date\nof this act, shall, within ten days of such effective date, transmit\nany and all information contained in the applications for such\nidentification, which remains in the possession of such city, to the\nDivision of Homeland Security and Emergency Services and the Office\nfor New Americans.\n\nSection three establishes the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nIn January 2015, New York City implemented the issuance of IDNYC, a\ngovernment issued identification card, obtainable by all New York City\nresidents, despite immigration status. In fact, the application for\nsuch card does not require disclosure of an individual's immigration\nstatus. IDNYC cards allow individuals the ability to establish bank\naccounts and access other city provided services. However, the\nprogram's enacting language includes provisions to allow the City to\ndestroy these records in an effort to evade the enforcement of federal\nimmigration laws.\n\nDestruction of these files would substantially jeopardize the health\nand safety of all New York residents. Failure to maintain records\ncould impede law enforcements' ability to conduct criminal\ninvestigations, and conduct effective counter terrorism measures. For\nexample, without the identifying information of these individuals, law\nenforcement would be unable to investigate money-laundering\n\nenterprises, which seek to fund potential terrorist attacks and other\nillicit activities.\n\nThis bill would prevent the City from destroying the IDNYC\napplications, and all information contained therein, as well as\nrequire that all the information be sent to the Office for New\nAmericans and Division of Homeland Security for their records.\nProviding the Office for New Americans with this information will\nconnect individuals with an additional resource at the state level to\nseek assistance and obtain services. Additionally, the funds collected\nfrom the fine imposed will be allocated to the Office of New Americans\nin order to provide the Office with additional resources to enhance\ncurrent programs, and provide necessary assistance to those in need.\nThe Division of Homeland Security will maintain a copy of the records\nin the event of a criminal investigation. The State's interest in\nprotecting the safety of all New York State residents is far greater\nthan that of any city attempt to evade federal immigration laws.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew Bill\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
